File: force-app/main/default/triggers/OpportunityTrigger.trigger
```java
/**
 * Single-entry-point trigger for Opportunity.
 * All business logic lives in the Opportunities domain class.
 */
trigger OpportunityTrigger on Opportunity (
    before insert,
    before update,
    before delete,
    after insert,
    after update,
    after delete,
    after undelete
) {
    fflib_SObjectDomain.triggerHandler(Opportunities.class);
}
```

### How it works

| Concern | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Dispatch** | `fflib_SObjectDomain.triggerHandler(Opportunities.class)` inspects `Trigger.new/old`, determines the event (e.g. `BEFORE_INSERT`), instantiates the domain via the inner `Constructor`, and calls the matching hook (`beforeInsert()`, `afterUpdate()`, …). |
| **Defaults on create only** | The logic lives inside `beforeInsert()`, which the framework only invokes when `Trigger.isInsert`. Updates never hit this code path. |
| **Existing values preserved** | Both defaults are guarded by a null / blank check, so a pre-populated `CloseDate` or `Type` is left untouched. |
| **Bulk-safe** | The `for` loop iterates the entire `records` list in a single pass; no SOQL or DML inside the loop. |
| **Bulk-safe trigger** | The trigger body is a single delegation call, so every execution (all 7 events) routes through one path—easy to unit-test and to disable via custom settings if fflib's `IDispatcher` is later wired in. |

